    <header>
      <p class="term-category">Battery Chemistries</p>
      <h1>{term}</h1>
      <p class="term-summary">
        <strong>Lithium-ion (Li-ion)</strong> is the dominant rechargeable
        battery chemistry in modern consumer and industrial electronics.
        Energy is stored by moving lithium ions between a graphite anode and
        a metal-oxide cathode through a lithium-salt electrolyte.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Chemistry variants</h2>
      <p>
        "Lithium-ion" is a family, not a single chemistry — the cathode
        material defines the sub-type, and each sub-type has distinct energy
        density, cycle life, thermal stability, and cost:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>LCO</strong> (Lithium Cobalt Oxide): highest energy density; laptops, older smartphones. Cobalt-heavy.</li>
        <li><strong>NMC</strong> (Nickel Manganese Cobalt): balanced; modern laptops, EVs.</li>
        <li><strong>NCA</strong> (Nickel Cobalt Aluminum): high energy; Tesla and premium EVs.</li>
        <li><strong>LFP</strong> (Lithium Iron Phosphate): safer, longer cycle life, lower energy density; stationary storage, some EVs, LED torches.</li>
        <li><strong>LTO</strong> (Lithium Titanate): very long cycle life, fast charge, low energy density; industrial applications.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>End-of-life hazards</h2>
      <p>
        Damaged, punctured, or over-heated Li-ion cells can enter{" "}
        <a href="/encyclopedia/thermal-runaway-lithium/">thermal runaway</a>{" "}
        — a self-sustaining exothermic reaction that vents flammable
        electrolyte vapor and can propagate to adjacent cells. Common
        triggers at end-of-life: physical damage in transport,
        short-circuit from mixed disposal with other batteries, moisture
        contact with exposed terminals, and pressure from compaction in
        general-waste truck compressors.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the reason Li-ion batteries and Li-ion-containing devices
        (laptops, phones, power banks, wireless earphones with sealed
        batteries) must never be placed in general household waste — the
        compaction cycle alone can trigger fires.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Recycling routes</h2>
      <p>
        Li-ion recycling separates into three broad technology families:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pyrometallurgy</strong> — high-temperature smelting recovers cobalt, nickel, copper as an alloy. Lithium is often lost to slag.</li>
        <li><strong>Hydrometallurgy</strong> — dissolution in acid or alkali, followed by selective precipitation. Better lithium recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Direct recycling</strong> — physical separation preserving the cathode structure. Highest theoretical value; less mature at commercial scale.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A given Li-ion battery in India typically routes through mechanical
        shredding (to expose materials), then to specialised recyclers using
        pyrometallurgical or hydrometallurgical processes. Recovery
        percentages vary widely by chemistry and process — cobalt
        recoveries of 90%+ are commercially routine, lithium recoveries
        historically much lower but improving.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Disposal in India</h2>
      <p>
        Li-ion battery disposal in India falls under the{" "}
        <a href="/encyclopedia/batteries-waste-management-rules-2022/">
          Batteries (Waste Management) Rules, 2022
        </a>, which applies EPR obligations to battery producers. For
        consumers and bulk users, the practical route is authorised
        recycler pickup — informal-sector routes exist but often end in
        environmentally destructive processing.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Kochi, Ewaste Kochi provides doorstep pickup for Li-ion
        batteries and Li-ion-containing devices. See{" "}
        <a href="/battery-recycling/">battery recycling</a> for the service,
        or{" "}
        <a href="/battery-recycling/laptop-battery-disposal-in-kerala-storage-safety-and-recycling/">
          laptop battery disposal
        </a>{" "}
        for the most common Li-ion category. Batteries are Kochi-doorstep
        only — dangerous-goods courier restrictions prevent pan-India
        ship-in for battery categories.
      </p>
    </section>

